\f1\fs22 Halo had seen her first, dammit, before Alexander or someone had sullied her to the point of conception. He doubted Shakti had performed the act. Maybe he could get Sarah to whisper the name of the meat sack sperm depositor as a bargain to spare her life\'97with her last breath, of course.\
Whichever way you dice her, Alexander Shakti was not worthy of the trophy but, spoiled as she was, Halo was determined to finish off the game.\
Halo fondled his Point of Order as he explored the corridors and open spaces of the facility. At five-ish on Christmas morning the staff was skeletal, and it was easy for Halo to avoid encounters; if any of them had but a glimpse of him, he presented well enough to appear as \'93geek dressed by People and Culture\'94.\
Halo had researched the facility thoroughly, and knew there\'92d be no obvious signposting to anywhere, but he didn\'92t believe there wouldn\'92t be some kind of hobo code to identify departments, routes and rooms for employees, and bamboozle guests and invaders. He cleared his mind of clutter, especially the expectation of arriving anywhere, and let his senses gather unfiltered. Sooner or later, the pattern he knew must be encoded somewhere would present itself for recognition.\
It was possible to see only so far down the corridors\'97each was a single curve that tightened or loosened as Halo followed them. Intersections and terminuses tended to appear suddenly, and Halo began to get a sense that they were laid out out as a series of intersecting and concentric ellipses that were influenced by the density of the rock and the presence of underground caves.\
There were no room numbers; no icons; no stripes on the floor; no exposed pipes, conduits or cable trays to carry a colour; no change in wall colour or texture; no change in lighting temperature. Sprinkler caps and fire extinguisher cabinets were uniform. The floor was dead level; the grout between the uniform floor tiles remained unchanged. Halo sensed no difference in temperature or humidity. The open spaces, though they varied in size, could have been cloned from the concourse, even down to the water features and tropical fish. The tree ferns were the same collection of species. There were no more wallabies.\
The facility was the very antithesis of industry or commerce; it was aesthetically pleasing, and minimalist without being austere. It felt welcoming and warm, almost homey. The only decorative features appeared at the corners of corridor junctions where coloured strips, maybe twelve centimetres by two, stacked like rungs on ladders with a centimetre between. At each corner there was a ladder on each wall. The colours were muted and warm\'97even the green, Halo noted\'97\
\'93Excuse me.\'94 A young woman had somehow snuck up on him.\
Halo turned, smiled at the woman and let his empathy tech do the work. The woman responded unconsciously and quickly\'97pupils dilated, breathing deepened, skin tones became firmer. For a moment, Halo considered taking her. On any other day, she\'92d make a perfect engagement.\
\'93What is your colour?\'94 she asked, and beamed, as if she was proud to be playing a game for the first time.\
\'93Green,\'94 Halo answered instinctively.\
\'93Green?\'94 The girl blurted, and Halo thought he might have to make sport of her after all. But then she went on, \'93Typical! You security guys come down on us if we don\'92t use the correct name, and then you go and say 
\f2\i green
\f1\i0 ?\'94 She was already flirting. \'93Typical man.\'94\
\'93Then what colour is it?\'94 Halo toyed while his empathy tech had her brushing her hair back.\
\'93Avocado,\'94 she answered.\
Halo nodded as a reward for her answer. \'93And what colour are you?\'94\
\'93Aubergine.\'94\
\'93Ah,\'94 Halo said.\
The girl found she needed to keep the conversation going. \'93Yeah, I know, right? Christmas morning, but\'85\'94 She stopped herself short of blurting the reason for being there and laughed nervously.\
Halo laughed too, just enough. \'93Well\'85\'94\
The girl stepped to one side, assuming the corridor Halo would need. \'93I won\'92t keep you. Merry Christmas.\'94\
\'93Merry Christmas.\'94 Halo watched the girl walk. She glanced over her shoulder, caught him following her with his eyes, and smiled. Once she\'92d disappeared around the arc of the corridor, Halo examined the colour codes at the two corners of the T-junction. Both corners confirmed that avocado\'97Security\'97was down the corridor the girl had indicated by body language, along with another six departments. On the continuance of the corridor he\'92d come from was a purple \'97aubergine\'97strip.\
Halo retraced his steps and quickly confirmed the Traverse station was a creamy yellow-orange. What would they call that? Sandstone?\
The colours were too elegant, too harmonious. Halo decided they\'92d been inspired by 
\f2\i Ulyssian House & Mansion
\f1\i0 , selected by interior decorators, and commissioned personally by Eloise with armoires full of money. Halo would have preferred a hobo code carved in, something he could read with his fingertips as well as his eyes.\
Where was Sarah in this sprawling installation? She\'92d rescued Eloise from Alexander, so it followed that she\'92d still be with Eloise, and Eloise would be in the safest, most fortified part of the installation. Halo wouldn\'92t be surprised\'97surprise being one of Halo\'92s least favourite emotions\'97if Eloise had a personal bunker down here somewhere.\
Halo heard voices, and swiftly positioned himself close to where he could hear and still remain out of sight beyond the curves of the corridors. \
He found two junior nerds in lab coats, and followed. They were positing ideas about why a washing machine had \'93woken up\'94. They were both professionally and geekily excited about what that might mean. They moaned about someone they called The Johnny and another called Dr Ortolan and found neither to be intellectually up to their roles in Project \'85 Cobweb? They joked about whether The Johnny was the father of the boss\'92s baby.\
\'93I swear, I keep expecting Sarah to pop her sprog right there in the Ironing Room.\'94
